# November 23, 2026: Northern & Downeast Maine (region men, claimed by tools/choose.py --thinnest; it held 2 places).
# First scatter across Washington, Hancock, Waldo and Aroostook counties: the state's historic forts and blockhouses,
# a tavern, a lighthouse, a railroad station, a dam, an Acadian village and two Houlton landmarks. Facts come from the
# linked sources, opened in this session: Wikipedia read as raw text (wiki_raw.py), the Maine Bureau of Parks and Lands
# pages for each fort (maine.gov/fortknox and the like), the museums' own pages, the National Park Service page for the
# Acadian Village and the American Lighthouse Foundation's pages for Little River Light.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
BATCH={'key':'men_hqzv','label':'Downeast and northern Maine: forts, a tavern, a lighthouse and Acadian buildings','added':'2026-11-23 16:32'}
FEES=['Maine state park day-use fees','https://www.maine.gov/DACF/parks/park_passes_fees_rules/park_day_use_fees.shtml']
PLACES=[
N('fort-knox-prospect','Fort Knox','Prospect','men','fort','standing',44.56619,-68.80242,'exact','open','walk','forts','1844–1869',
  'A large granite fort on the west bank of the Penobscot River, built from 1844 and never fully finished once money for the masonry stopped in 1869. It was the first fort in Maine made wholly of granite, cut from Mount Waldo upriver, and it was built because of the border scares of the Aroostook War and the memory of the British raids of 1779 and 1814. It never saw combat. It was garrisoned in the Civil War and the Spanish-American War, when about 500 Connecticut troops camped outside it in tents.',
  'The fort is described by the state as an unaltered example of a mid-19th-century granite coastal fort. Friends of Fort Knox have repaired the masonry and the Battery A powder magazine, restored the officers\' quarters, opened the enlisted men\'s quarters and a cistern, and put four 24-pound flank howitzers on display. The old torpedo storage shed is now the visitor centre. Interiors are open in season; the extent of the original interior fabric was not checked.',
  'Maine\'s first granite fort, with its barracks, magazines and passages still there.',
  'Fort Knox State Historic Site, 711 Fort Knox Road, Prospect, run by the Maine Bureau of Parks and Lands with the Friends of Fort Knox. Parking at the site. Fee charged ($6 Maine residents, $8 non-residents on the state fee table); the observatory tower of the Penobscot Narrows Bridge is sold separately. Off season, visitors may park outside the gate and walk in. Hours from the state\'s own page for the site.',
  [W('Fort Knox (Maine)'),['Fort Knox State Historic Site (Maine Bureau of Parks and Lands)','https://www.maine.gov/fortknox'],FEES],
  hours='May 1 to October 31, daily 9 a.m. to sunset; off season grounds only, park outside the gate',th=[44.56592,-68.80381],thn='Fort Knox lot, Fort Knox Road',brick='no'),
N('fort-pownall-stockton-springs','Fort Pownall earthworks, Fort Point State Park','Stockton Springs','men','fort','traces',44.46812,-68.81175,'site','public','walk','forts','1759–1775',
  'A British fort of 1759, named for Governor Thomas Pownall of Massachusetts, on the high ground at the eastern tip of Cape Jellison. It had an eight-pointed earthwork, a ditch and a two-storey timber blockhouse, and never fought. In 1775 the British took its cannon and powder, and Continental soldiers later burned the blockhouse and filled in most of the ditches.',
  'Only the earthworks and stone foundations remain. The state\'s page says the remains carry interpretive panels and a stone marking the first burial place of General Samuel Waldo. The park around it has picnic sites, a fishing pier, trails and the Fort Point Light Station.',
  '',
  'Fort Point State Park, run by the Maine Bureau of Parks and Lands, off Route 1 in Stockton Springs about three miles down the peninsula. Fee charged ($3 Maine residents, $4 non-residents). Park outside the gate in the off season and walk in. Hours from the state\'s own page for the park.',
  [W('Fort Pownall'),['Fort Point State Park (Maine Bureau of Parks and Lands)','https://www.maine.gov/fortpoint'],FEES],
  hours='May 15 to October 15, daily 9 a.m. to sunset; off season park outside the gate and walk in',conf='mixed',th=[44.46928,-68.81190],thn='Fort Point State Park lot, State Park Road',brick='no'),
N('fort-george-castine','Fort George earthworks, Castine','Castine','men','fort','traces',44.39083,-68.80556,'site','public','walk','forts','1779–1815',
  'A palisaded earth fort built by the British in 1779 on the high ground of the Bagaduce peninsula, as the centre of a planned colony called New Ireland. It held out against the Massachusetts fleet in the Penobscot Expedition that summer, a failed attempt to retake Castine. The British left in 1784, came back in the War of 1812 from September 1814 to April 1815, and rebuilt the fort then.',
  'A roughly square earthwork about 200 feet on a side, with corner bastions that project another 40 feet. Most of the banks stand about 10 feet high and the east bastion about 20. The palisade, moat and gateway are gone. It sits in a 7-acre park.',
  '',
  'Owned by the State of Maine and kept up by the Town of Castine, according to Wikipedia; the park is reached on foot from the town streets. No managing page was read for hours or parking, so hours are not checked.',
  [W('Fort George (Castine, Maine)')],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('fort-obrien-machiasport','Fort O\'Brien State Historic Site','Machiasport','men','fort','traces',44.68980,-67.39582,'site','public','walk','forts','1775–1863',
  'The Machias River fort named for Jeremiah O\'Brien, who led the colonists who took the British ship Margaretta in June 1775. A four-gun battery was built here after that fight and destroyed by the British in 1777. American troops held the site in the War of 1812, and the British burned it in 1814. A five-gun battery was added in 1863 and never fired. The federal government gave the land to Maine in 1923.',
  'Earthworks of the Civil War battery and a flagpole mark the lawn above the river; the Revolutionary earthworks just north are overgrown. A Civil War 12-pound Napoleon cannon stands there. There is a small parking area and a picnic area.',
  '',
  'State historic site run by the Maine Bureau of Parks and Lands, on Route 92 five miles south of Machias. Fee charged ($3 Maine residents, $4 non-residents). Off season park outside the gate and walk in. Hours from the state\'s own page for the site (Wikipedia says the season ends on Labor Day; the state page says September 30).',
  [W('Fort O\'Brien'),['Fort O\'Brien State Historic Site (Maine Bureau of Parks and Lands)','https://www.maine.gov/fortobrien'],FEES],
  hours='Memorial Day to September 30, daily 9 a.m. to sunset; off season park outside the gate and walk in',th=[44.68961,-67.39647],thn='Fort O\'Brien lot, Port Road',brick='no'),
N('burnham-tavern-machias','Burnham Tavern','Machias','men','hotel','standing',44.71416,-67.45953,'exact','open','walk','hotels','1770',
  'A gambrel-roofed wooden tavern built in 1770 by Job Burnham, and the only building in the Machias area with a documented link to the American Revolution. The local militia met here before the Battle of Machias in June 1775, and the wounded of both sides were treated in it afterwards. It is run as a museum by a chapter of the Daughters of the American Revolution.',
  'A two-storey frame house with a large central chimney, a split staircase in the entry and a big kitchen fireplace. Rooms hold furniture of the Revolutionary period and objects from the capture of the Margaretta. Entry is by guided tour of the interior.',
  'The 1770 tavern where Machias planned the first naval fight of the Revolution.',
  'Owned and run by the Burnham Tavern Museum (the Hannah Weston Chapter, DAR). A $5 donation is suggested for adults, $2 for students. Docents are volunteers: the museum asks visitors to phone first to confirm a guide is there. Street parking in downtown Machias. Hours from the museum\'s own tours page; a 2026 notice on the same site gave weekday tours from July 7 to August 28, which was only seen in a search result.',
  [W('Burnham Tavern'),['Burnham Tavern Museum: tours','https://www.burnhamtavern.com/tours/']],
  hours='Generally July and August, Monday to Friday 10 a.m. to 3 p.m. when a guide is free; call first. Other months by appointment',brick='no'),
N('fort-sullivan-eastport','Fort Sullivan site and magazine ruin, Eastport','Eastport','men','fort','ruins',44.9074,-66.98795,'area','view','walk','forts','1808–1818',
  'A US Army fort begun in 1808 or 1809 on Clark\'s Hill above Eastport, with a four-gun circular earthwork, a wooden blockhouse and barracks. The British took it in July 1814 and called it Fort Sherbrooke, and they held Eastport until it was returned to the United States on June 30, 1818. The fort was still used as a coast defence in the 19th century.',
  'Wikipedia says the ruins of an old powder magazine off McKinley Street are the only remains of the fort still in place, and that one early 19th-century officers\' quarters has become the Barracks Museum, run by the Border Historical Society and open in July and August. Neither was checked on the ground, and the pin is the general site of the fort.',
  '',
  'Not checked who owns the magazine ruin; look from the public street. Barracks Museum days and times were not found on a page of the society\'s own (a search gave only "July and August"), so hours are not checked.',
  [W('Fort Sullivan (Maine)')],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('little-river-light-cutler','Little River Light','Cutler','men','light','standing',44.65088,-67.1921,'exact','boat','boat','','1846–1888',
  'A light station on Little River Island at the mouth of Little River, begun in 1846. The present iron tower went up in 1876, built by the Army Corps of Engineers, and the keeper\'s house in 1888. The boathouse is of 1881 and the oilhouse of 1905. It was automated in 1975, and the American Lighthouse Foundation has leased it since 2001, restored the tower and relit the lantern.',
  'The 35-foot iron tower has a steel and brick frame and an octagonal lantern. The keeper\'s house, brick oilhouse and a wooden boathouse linked by a boardwalk also stand. The light is not seen from the shore, but the island can be glimpsed from Cutler.',
  'One of the few iron lighthouses in Maine, on an island you reach by boat.',
  'On an island reached by boat only. The Friends of Little River Lighthouse run boats from the town landing in Cutler on scheduled open-house days (no admission fee; donations asked), and the keeper\'s house is rented for overnight stays. Open-house dates were not listed on the page read, so hours are not checked: see littleriverlight.org or phone 877-276-4682. Do not walk on the private piers in Cutler.',
  [W('Little River Light'),['American Lighthouse Foundation: tour Little River Lighthouse','https://lighthousefoundation.org/what-we-do/programs/lighthouse-tours/tour-little-river-light/'],['Friends of Little River Lighthouse: directions','https://www.littleriverlight.org/directions']],
  hours='Open-house days only, with a boat from Cutler; dates not checked',brick='some'),
N('fort-kent-blockhouse','Fort Kent blockhouse','Fort Kent','men','fort','standing',47.25182,-68.59413,'exact','open','walk','forts','1838–1843',
  'The only surviving fort from the Aroostook War, the border quarrel with New Brunswick, built from 1838 on a rise where the Fish River meets the Saint John and named for Governor Edward Kent. It had no earthworks or stockade; the blockhouse stood with a cookhouse, stables and, after 1839, barracks and officers\' quarters. General Winfield Scott and New Brunswick\'s lieutenant governor eased the crisis before the 1842 treaty. It is a National Historic Landmark.',
  'A two-storey hand-hewn cedar blockhouse, 23 feet square, whose upper floor overhangs the lower by 15 inches, with a pyramidal roof, dormers and rows of rifle ports. The inside has been altered for heating, plumbing and museum displays. It is open to visitors in season.',
  'The last blockhouse of the Aroostook War, on the Canadian border.',
  'Fort Kent State Historic Site, run by the Maine Bureau of Parks and Lands from Aroostook State Park (207-768-8341), off Route 1 (West Main Street). Fee charged ($2 Maine residents, $3 non-residents). Off season park outside the gate and walk in. Hours from the state\'s own page for the site.',
  [W('Fort Kent (fort)'),['Fort Kent State Historic Site (Maine Bureau of Parks and Lands)','https://www.maine.gov/fortkent'],FEES],
  hours='Memorial Day to September 30, daily 9 a.m. to sunset; off season park outside the gate and walk in',brick='no'),
N('acadian-village-van-buren','Acadian Village','Van Buren','men','town','standing',47.20474,-67.98837,'site','open','drive','','1780s–1875',
  'An open-air museum of the Acadian families who settled the Saint John Valley in the 1780s. The buildings were moved or built here: the National Park Service counts 17, and Wikipedia describes six on the National Register, five mid-19th-century wooden buildings (three houses, a schoolhouse of 1875 moved from Hamlin and a blacksmith shop) and a reproduction log church. The sources count the buildings differently.',
  'The Morneault House is a log house covered in clapboards that once served as an early post office, and its ship-knee supports, like those of the Acadian barn, show the shipbuilding skill of the settlers. Visitors walk through the buildings on the bank of the Saint John River.',
  '',
  'Run by Notre Héritage Vivant (Our Living Heritage), 859 Main Street (US Route 1), Van Buren, phone 207-868-5042. Admission $10 adults and $5 students on the National Park Service page, which also lists the village as part of Maine Acadian Culture. Parking on site. Season from the Park Service page; daily times were not on that page, so only the season is given.',
  [W('Acadian Village (Van Buren, Maine)'),['National Park Service: Acadian Village','https://www.nps.gov/maac/planyourvisit/acadvillage.htm']],
  hours='Mid-June to mid-September; daily times not checked',conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('blackhawk-putnam-tavern-houlton','Blackhawk Putnam Tavern','Houlton','men','hotel','standing',46.12761,-67.84267,'exact','view','walk','hotels','1813',
  'Built in 1813 by master carpenter Samuel Wormwood for Aaron Putnam, and the oldest standing building in Aroostook County. It probably became a tavern after the military road reached Houlton in 1828, and held sessions of the Washington County and then the Aroostook County court. Blackhawk Putnam, who led a company of Maine volunteers in the Civil War, was the son of a later owner.',
  'A Federal house of two full storeys under a side-gabled roof, five bays wide, with two interior brick chimneys and a Colonial Revival porch added in the 20th century. Inside it has a centre hall and a mix of Federal and Greek Revival trim. It is now a private home and the base of a social services agency, so the interior is not open to visitors.',
  '',
  'Private property at 22 North Street (US Route 1); look from the public sidewalk. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Blackhawk Putnam Tavern')],
  brick='some'),
N('market-square-historic-district-houlton','Market Square Historic District, Houlton','Houlton','men','town','standing',46.12611,-67.84,'area','public','walk','','1885–1910',
  'Houlton\'s business centre, rebuilt after fires in 1884 and 1902 when the Bangor and Aroostook Railroad arrived in 1893 and set off a building boom. The result is a single-period downtown of brick and masonry blocks of two to four storeys, designed between 1885 and 1910, many by Bangor architect Wilfred E. Mansur.',
  'Buildings to look at: the granite-fronted First National Bank of 1907 on Market Square, the Romanesque Revival post office and custom house on Water Street, a Mansur-designed fire station and the Colonial Revival Elks Lodge at the east end of Main Street. These are viewed from the streets; whether any interior is open was not checked.',
  '',
  'Streets and sidewalks in the centre of Houlton; park in Market Square. The buildings are in many ownerships and are not open as a group.',
  [W('Market Square Historic District (Houlton, Maine)')],
  brick='yes'),
N('calais-observatory-meridian-park','Calais Observatory, Meridian Park','Calais','men','curio','standing',45.18476,-67.28076,'exact','view','walk','','1857–1866',
  'Two granite stones on a ledge on the old Calais Academy grounds where US Coast Survey crews fixed the longitude of Calais in 1857 and again in 1866, by exchanging telegraph time signals with Cambridge, Massachusetts, and with Greenwich. In 1866 it was arguably one of the most precisely located places in the country. It was listed on the National Register in 2012.',
  'A 3-foot transit stone with channels cut in its top, a taller tapering clock stone about 2 feet to its west, rough square pads cut in the bedrock beside it and two drilled holes in the ledge. A NOAA plaque marks it. The shelter that once covered it is gone.',
  '',
  'Meridian Park at the corner of North and Lincoln Streets, Calais, on the former Calais Academy grounds. Who owns and keeps the park was not checked, so look from the street. Parking on the streets.',
  [W('Calais Observatory')],
  brick='no'),
N('machias-railroad-station','Machias Railroad Station','Machias','men','rail','standing',44.71952,-67.45164,'exact','open','walk','stations','1898–1985',
  'A station of 1898, enlarged in 1910, built for the Washington County Railroad, one of five of its 24 stations that survive. The line was never profitable and ran under lease to the Maine Central from 1903; it was a freight depot from 1957 and the track was abandoned in 1985. The town owns the building and the chamber of commerce restored it in 2015 as a visitor centre and meeting space.',
  'A long single-storey wooden building with deep eaves on chamfered braces and a three-sided ticket booth on the track side, with a working signal beside it. It kept most of its original interior woodwork. The old right-of-way next to it is now the Downeast Sunrise Trail.',
  '',
  'Owned by the Town of Machias and leased to the chamber of commerce, on US Route 1 on the east edge of downtown, with the Downeast Sunrise Trail just behind it. Opening days and times were not found, so hours are not checked.',
  [W('Machias station')],
  hours='?',conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('ellsworth-power-house-and-dam','Ellsworth Power House and Dam','Ellsworth','men','industry','standing',44.54435,-68.43002,'site','view','drive','','1907',
  'A hydroelectric plant on the Union River just north of downtown Ellsworth, built in 1907 by the Bar Harbor and Union River Power Company to the designs of its engineer, James Leonard. It was one of the first peaking power plants in Maine, and its dam was once called the highest power dam in New England. It is still a working plant.',
  'A 71-foot concrete buttress dam across a gorge between two bluffs, with a Renaissance Revival power house at its west end built of concrete blocks, round-arched windows, a stone belt course, Palladian gable windows and a red tile roof. The dam was filled with concrete in the 1990s. Not open to the public.',
  '',
  'An operating utility, owned by a private hydropower company (Brookfield in 2014, per Wikipedia); look from public ground. Where to park and view was not checked.',
  [W('Ellsworth Power House and Dam')],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
]
